{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-categorical-fourier-unique","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:categorical-fourier-unique","kind":"proposition","title":"Uniqueness of the categorical Fourier transform","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-categorical-fourier-exists","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-categorical-laplace-unique"],"proofs":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-categorical-fourier-unique"],"tags":["uniqueness","fourier"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.CategoricalFourier.unique","text":"categorical-fourier-transform-unique.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/categorical-fourier-transform-unique.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:categorical-fourier-unique\"></a><p>The categorical Fourier transform <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{F}</code>, once it exists, is unique up to natural monoidal isomorphism: any two functors <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{F}</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{F}'</code> that intertwine the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-convolution over the algebra <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{A}</code> with the pointwise product on the spectrum of irreducible representations and preserve the Peter–Weyl projections are equivalent.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/categorical-fourier-transform-unique.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"3.20.16"}
